197:
“This is suffering”—whatever state is so called, that truth of suffering is real because it is according to its true nature. For suffering alone is suffering. It is unerring (avitatha) because there is no deviation from the stated nature. Indeed, suffering never becomes non-suffering (adukkha). It is not otherwise (ananyatha) because it is devoid of the nature of other truths. For suffering does not have the nature of the origin (samudaya), etc. The same method applies to the origin and the others.

## 1. Explanation of the First Suttanta Exposition

'Tathaṭṭha' means in the sense of having a true nature. The sense of oppression (pīḷanaṭṭha) and so on are exactly the meanings already stated in the Discourse on Knowledge (Ñāṇakathā).

'Ekappaṭivedhāni' means they are called so because they are penetrated by a single Path-knowledge (magga-ñāṇa), or because they are penetrated together (simultaneously). 'Anattaṭṭha' means in the sense of non-self, because all four truths are devoid of a self. Regarding this, it is stated in the Visuddhimagga: “In the ultimate sense, all truths should be understood as empty, because there is no one who feels, no one who acts, no one who is extinguished, and no one who goes.” Therefore, it is said:

“For there is suffering, but none who suffers;
No doer is found, but only the deed is there;
Cessation is, but not the man who is extinguished;
The path exists, but no traveler is seen.”

Or else:

“The first two truths are empty of permanence, beauty, happiness, and self;
The deathless state (Nibbāna) is empty of self;
The path is devoid of permanence, happiness, and self;
Thus, emptiness (suññatā) should be understood in those four truths.”

'Saccaṭṭha' means in the sense of being non-deceptive. 'Paṭivedhaṭṭha' means in the sense of being what should be penetrated at the moment of the Path. 'Ekasaṅgahitāni' means they are collected by each single meaning such as the sense of reality (tathaṭṭha), etc.; the meaning is that they come to a single reckoning. That which is collected as one is 'unity' (ekatta); the meaning is that because it is collected by one, it is called unity. Although the truths are many in their fourfold division, the singular form is used as 'yam ekasaṅgahitaṃ' in consideration of their unity. 'Penetrating unity with a single knowledge' means that one who stands having well-defined the diversity and unity of the four truths in the preliminary stage of the Path...

