"Buddhayeva" means they are indeed Buddhas (including Paccekabuddhas). "Agataṃ" means that which has never been reached before in the beginningless Saṃsāra. "Disā" (Direction/Destination) is so called because it is rightly reached through the entirety of the practice, or because all Buddhas speak of it as the supreme happiness, or because all suffering is abandoned by means of that Nibbāna.

Regarding the "Eightfold Path" (Aṭṭhaṅgika Magga) which is the way to that Nibbāna, what does it mean? It means that there is a collection of states having eight factors; that collection is called "Path" (Magga) in the sense of going, because one goes to Nibbāna by means of it. "Of the various recluses and brahmins, of the doctrines of others" means [superior to the paths] of the various recluses and brahmins who hold other views outside this Dispensation. "Aggo" (Foremost) means it is distinguished above their remaining paths. "Seṭṭho" (Best) means it is extremely praiseworthy compared to the remaining paths. "Mokkho" (Chief) means it is good in the forefront; the meaning is that this path alone is good in the presence of the remaining paths. "Uttamo" (Supreme) means it is highly transcendent over the remaining paths. "Pavaro" (Excellent) means it is to be honored in various ways above the remaining paths. The word "iti" is a particle used in the sense of cause. Therefore, it should be understood that the Blessed One taught: "Of paths, the Eightfold Path is the best." For it was said by the Blessed One:

"Of paths, the Eightfold Path is the best;
Of truths, the four terms (Four Noble Truths) are the best;
Of states, Dispassion is the best;
Of bipeds, the One with Vision (the Buddha) is the best."
(Dhammapada 273).

That verse is excerpted here and stated as "Of paths, the Eightfold Path is the best." In the remaining cycles also, the meaning should be understood by this method and by the method stated below.

In the terms beginning with "Dassana-virāga" (Dispassion through Vision), the dispassion called vision is "Dassana-virāga". It should be understood that here, Power (Bala) is mentioned before Faculty (Indriya) because the nature of Power is more distinguished than the nature of Faculty. The statement "Indriyas in the sense of predominance" etc., is only an explanation of the meaning of faculties etc., not of dispassion. By the term "Truths in the sense of reality," Truth-knowledge (Sacca-ñāṇa) should be understood. "Purification of Virtue" (Sīlavisuddhi) refers to Right Speech, Right Action, and Right Livelihood. "Purification of Mind" (Cittavisuddhi) refers to Right Concentration. "Purification of View" (Diṭṭhivisuddhi) refers to Right View and Right Intention. "In the sense of liberation" means in the sense of being liberated from the defilements to be abandoned by the respective paths. "Vijjā" (Knowledge) is Right View. "Vimutti" (Liberation) is Liberation by Uprooting (samuccheda-vimutti). "The Path is Nibbāna which is plunged into the Deathless in the sense of culmination" means it is called "Path" (magga) because it is sought by the Path, Fruit, and Reviewing knowledge.

All the states taught in this Description of Dispassion (Virāganiddesa) occur only at the Path-moment. In the Description of Liberation (Vimuttiniddesa), however, they occur at the Fruit-moment. Therefore, zeal (chanda) and attention (manasikāra) are also associated with the Path and Fruit.
